def add(n,topic,prompt,opts,ans,hints,sol,theory,ex,status='reviewed',note='',track='Mathematics',difficulty='Moderate'):
 w.add(n,track,topic,difficulty,prompt,opts,ans,hints,sol,theory,ex,status,note)
add(21,'Linear Algebra','Consider statements I and II. I: There exists N∈M₄(ℝ) with row-space basis {(1,1,1,−1),(1,−1,1,1)} and (1,2,1,4)∈Null(N). II: There exists M∈M₄(ℝ) with column-space basis {(1,1,1,0)ᵀ,(1,0,1,1)ᵀ} and (1,1,1,1)ᵀ,(1,0,1,0)ᵀ∈Null(M). Which is true?', ['I true and II false','Both false','Both true','I true and II false'],'',['A null vector must be perpendicular to every row; compute both dot products in I.','For II, choose a map whose kernel is the span of the two prescribed vectors and whose range is the prescribed two-dimensional space.'],'I is false: (1,−1,1,1)·(1,2,1,4)=4, so the proposed null vector is not perpendicular to the row space. II is true. Let c=(1,1,1,0)ᵀ and d=(1,0,1,1)ᵀ. The matrix with columns [c,d,−c,−d] has range span{c,d}; its action is (x₁−x₃)c+(x₂−x₄)d, which vanishes on both prescribed vectors. Thus I false, II true. Neither A/D, B nor C states this; A and D are duplicated. The source has no valid selectable answer.','For a real matrix, Null(A)=Row(A)⊥. This connects row space and kernel, but column space need not be perpendicular to the kernel of A: it is perpendicular to Null(Aᵀ). A linear transformation can be constructed by specifying its values on a basis. If desired kernel and range have dimensions k and r in an n-dimensional domain, rank–nullity requires k+r=n. Here the proposed kernel vectors are independent and the desired column vectors are independent, so both dimensions are two. Choosing two independent linear functionals that vanish on the kernel yields a direct construction. The common error is to apply the row-space orthogonality rule to the column space.',[('Can a matrix with row (1,2,3) annihilate (1,1,−1)?','Yes: the dot product is 1+2−3=0; the one-row matrix suffices.'),('Construct a map ℝ³→ℝ² with kernel span{(1,1,1)} and full range.','T(x,y,z)=(x−z,y−z). Its kernel is exactly the prescribed line and T(1,0,0),T(0,1,0) form a basis of ℝ².')],status='source_issue',note='The printed choices A and D are identical; the actual truth combination (I false, II true) is absent. No answer label assigned.')
add(23,'Linear Algebra','Let M=[[0,−1,0],[1,2,−1],[1,1,3]]. Given that 1 is an eigenvalue, which statement is true?',['−2 is an eigenvalue','3 is an eigenvalue','The eigenspace of each eigenvalue has dimension 1','M is diagonalizable'],'C',['Compute det(λI−M), using the zero in its first row.','Factor it as (λ−1)(λ−2)² and calculate rank(M−2I).'],'det(λI−M)=λ³−5λ²+8λ−4=(λ−1)(λ−2)². Thus −2 and 3 are not eigenvalues: A,B are false. M−2I has rows (−2,−1,0),(1,0,−1),(1,1,1); the first two are independent and the third is their negative sum, so rank=2 and dim E₂=1. The simple eigenvalue 1 also has eigenspace dimension 1. Therefore C is true. Only two independent eigenvectors exist, so D is false.','An eigenvalue λ is a root of the characteristic polynomial det(λI−A). Its algebraic multiplicity counts its repetitions as a root, while geometric multiplicity is dim ker(A−λI). Geometric multiplicity is at least one and never exceeds algebraic multiplicity. A matrix over a field containing its eigenvalues is diagonalizable exactly when the total dimensions of its eigenspaces equal its size, or equivalently when geometric and algebraic multiplicities agree for every eigenvalue. A repeated eigenvalue does not automatically prevent diagonalization: the decisive issue is its eigenspace. Rank–nullity computes that dimension without explicitly finding every eigenvector.',[('Is [[2,1],[0,2]] diagonalizable?','No: the sole eigenvalue 2 has multiplicity two but its eigenspace is span{(1,0)}, dimension one.'),('Is diag(2,2,3) diagonalizable?','Yes, it is already diagonal. E₂ has dimension two and E₃ dimension one.')],note='Top row of matrix recovered by viewing the full attached PDF page 1; question crop clips it.')
add(24,'Discrete Mathematics','S={1,…,100}, A={1,…,10}, B={41,…,50}. How many subsets of S meet both A and B?',['2¹⁰⁰/2²⁰','100!/(10!10!)','2⁸⁰(2¹⁰−1)²','2¹⁰⁰−2(2¹⁰)'],'C',['Partition S into A, B, and their 80-element complement.','Choose independently a nonempty subset of each 10-element set and any subset of the complement.'],'A and B are disjoint. There are 2¹⁰−1 nonempty choices in each, and 2⁸⁰ unrestricted choices outside A∪B. Multiplication gives 2⁸⁰(2¹⁰−1)², C. Equivalently inclusion–exclusion gives 2¹⁰⁰−2·2⁹⁰+2⁸⁰. A counts only unrestricted subsets of the complement; B is a factorial arrangement count; D removes the wrong families and omits their overlap.','An m-element set has 2ᵐ subsets, including the empty set. When restrictions concern disjoint parts of a set, choices in those parts are independent and the product rule applies. A nonempty restriction changes 2ᵐ to 2ᵐ−1. Alternatively, to count objects satisfying several requirements, subtract those violating each requirement and add back those violating both. For a subset to avoid a prescribed m-element region, all its choices must come from the remaining n−m elements, giving 2ⁿ⁻ᵐ possibilities. The sizes of bad families are exponential in the available elements, not in the forbidden elements. Overlap corrections are essential in inclusion–exclusion.',[('An 8-element set contains disjoint regions of sizes 2 and 3. Count subsets meeting both.','(2²−1)(2³−1)2³=168.'),('Count subsets of a 6-element set that contain at least one of two specified elements.','2⁶−2⁴=48: subtract subsets avoiding both.')])
add(25,'Linear Algebra','A is a 4×4 matrix with eigenvalues −1,1,1,−2. If B=A⁴−5A²+5I, find trace(A+B).',['0','−12','3','9'],'C',['For a polynomial p, the eigenvalues of p(A) are p(λ), counted with multiplicity.','Evaluate λ+λ⁴−5λ²+5 on −1,1,1,−2.'],'Let p(t)=t+t⁴−5t²+5. Then p(−1)=0, p(1)=2 (twice), and p(−2)=−1. The trace is 0+2+2−1=3, C; hence the other numeric choices fail. This calculation does not assume A is diagonalizable, since triangularization preserves the diagonal entries and trace.','The trace of a square matrix equals the sum of its eigenvalues counted with algebraic multiplicity. Over the complex numbers every matrix is similar to an upper triangular matrix, and applying a polynomial replaces each diagonal entry λ by p(λ). Consequently tr p(A)=Σp(λ). This remains valid for defective matrices with too few eigenvectors. Trace is invariant under similarity and linear in matrices, while it is not generally multiplicative: tr(AB) need not equal tr(A)tr(B). The constant term c in p contributes c times the matrix size, because it represents cI. Be careful to retain repeated eigenvalues when summing.',[('Eigenvalues of A are 1,2. Find tr(A²+I).','(1²+1)+(2²+1)=7.'),('A has eigenvalues −1,−1,3. Find tr(A³−A).','Two contributions (−1)³−(−1)=0 and one 27−3=24; total 24.')])
add(26,'Linear Algebra','For n>1, which is an inner product on the real vector space of n×n symmetric matrices?',['⟨A,B⟩=tr(A)tr(B)','⟨A,B⟩=tr(AB)','⟨A,B⟩=det(AB)','⟨A,B⟩=tr(A)+tr(B)'],'B',['An inner product must be bilinear, symmetric, and positive definite.','For symmetric A, tr(A²)=Σᵢⱼaᵢⱼ². Test A=diag(1,−1,0,…,0) for choice A.'],'B is bilinear and tr(AB)=tr(BA). Since Aᵀ=A, tr(A²)=Σᵢⱼaᵢⱼ²>0 for A≠0, so B is an inner product. A gives zero squared length to the nonzero traceless matrix diag(1,−1,0,…). C is not bilinear for n>1 and gives zero on nonzero singular matrices. D is not bilinear, e.g. ⟨0,B⟩=tr(B), which need not vanish.','A real inner product is symmetric, linear in each argument, and positive definite: ⟨v,v⟩>0 for v≠0. On matrices the Frobenius inner product is tr(AᵀB)=Σaᵢⱼbᵢⱼ. Restricting to symmetric matrices removes the transpose and yields tr(AB). Positivity must be checked on every nonzero member of the space, rather than only on identity matrices. A bilinear form built from one linear functional, such as tr(A)tr(B), has a large kernel whenever the space has dimension greater than one. Determinants are homogeneous of degree n rather than linear, so determinant-based formulas usually fail bilinearity.',[('Is tr(AᵀB) an inner product on all real rectangular matrices?','Yes: it is entrywise Euclidean dot product and tr(AᵀA)=Σaᵢⱼ².'),('Is tr(AB) positive definite on all real 2×2 matrices?','No. For A=[[0,−1],[1,0]], A²=−I and tr(A²)=−2.')])
add(27,'Real Analysis','aₙ=3+5(−1/2)ⁿ+(−1)ⁿ(1/4+(−1)ⁿ2/n). Find (liminf aₙ,limsup aₙ).',['(−2,8)','(11/4,13/4)','(3,5)','(1/4,7/4)'],'B',['Simplify the last product to (−1)ⁿ/4+2/n.','The geometric term and 2/n vanish; separate even and odd indices.'],'aₙ=3+5(−1/2)ⁿ+(−1)ⁿ/4+2/n. Along even indices the limit is 3+1/4=13/4; along odd indices it is 3−1/4=11/4. Every term belongs to one of these two subsequences, so these are the only accumulation values. Thus liminf=11/4 and limsup=13/4, B. A,C,D give different endpoints and are false.','For a bounded sequence, limsup is the limit of the decreasing sequence of tail suprema, and liminf is the limit of the increasing tail infima. They are also the largest and smallest subsequential limits. If a sequence splits into finitely many subsequences, each with a limit, its accumulation values are exactly those limits. Terms tending to zero do not change limsup or liminf when added to a bounded sequence. Alternating factors (−1)ⁿ therefore suggest separating parity, while a geometric factor rⁿ disappears whenever |r|<1. The initial terms can be large without affecting either limiting quantity: both definitions ignore every finite prefix.',[('Find liminf and limsup of 2+(−1)ⁿ+1/n.','Odd terms tend to 1 and even terms to 3; liminf=1, limsup=3.'),('Does (−1)ⁿ/n converge?','Yes, its absolute value is 1/n→0, so both liminf and limsup are 0.')])
add(28,'Real Analysis','f(x)=x² and g(x)=sin x on ℝ. Which function is uniformly continuous on ℝ?',['g(f(x))','g(x)f(x)','f(g(x))','f(x)+g(x)'],'C',['Write the choices as sin(x²), x²sin x, sin²x, and x²+sin x.','A bounded derivative proves uniform continuity for sin²x; use nearby large arguments to disprove the others.'],'C: |(sin²x)′|=|sin2x|≤1, hence it is Lipschitz. A fails: choose xₙ=√(2πn), yₙ=√(2πn+π/2). Their distance tends to zero while sin(yₙ²)−sin(xₙ²)=1. B fails with xₙ=2πn, yₙ=xₙ+1/xₙ²: the argument gap vanishes, but yₙ²sin yₙ→1 whereas xₙ²sin xₙ=0. D fails with xₙ=n,yₙ=n+1/n: the squares differ by 2+1/n², and the sine difference tends to zero.','Uniform continuity requires one δ that works for all points in the domain for a given ε. Ordinary continuity permits δ to depend on the point. A differentiable function with globally bounded derivative is Lipschitz by the mean-value theorem and hence uniformly continuous. An unbounded derivative alone does not disprove uniform continuity; an explicit pair of sequences at distances tending to zero with function differences bounded away from zero does. Composition preserves uniform continuity when both functions are uniformly continuous on the relevant domains. Here squaring is uniformly continuous on the bounded range [−1,1] of sine, despite failing uniform continuity on all of ℝ.',[('Is cos²x uniformly continuous on ℝ?','Yes: derivative −sin2x has absolute value at most 1.'),('Is x² uniformly continuous on ℝ?','No: xₙ=n,yₙ=n+1/n have distance→0 but yₙ²−xₙ²→2.')])
add(29,'Real Analysis','Let S₁=1/3−(1/2)(1/3²)+(1/3)(1/3³)−(1/4)(1/3⁴)+⋯ and S₂=1/4+(1/2)(1/4²)+(1/3)(1/4³)+(1/4)(1/4⁴)+⋯. Which identity is true?',['3S₁=4S₂','4S₁=3S₂','S₁+S₂=0','S₁=S₂'],'D',['Recognize the Taylor series for log(1+x) and −log(1−x).','Evaluate log(1+1/3) and −log(1−1/4).'],'For |x|<1, log(1+x)=Σₙ≥₁(−1)ⁿ⁺¹xⁿ/n and −log(1−x)=Σₙ≥₁xⁿ/n. Thus S₁=log(4/3), S₂=−log(3/4)=log(4/3), proving D. This common value is positive and nonzero; therefore unequal scaling factors in A,B cannot hold, and C would require it to be zero.','The logarithm series follows by integrating the geometric series 1/(1−t)=Σtⁿ from 0 to x inside its radius of convergence. This gives −log(1−x)=Σxⁿ/n for |x|<1; replacing x by −x gives log(1+x). Within the radius of convergence termwise integration is justified by uniform convergence on compact subintervals. Series with a reciprocal integer factor often arise from integrating geometric series. The sign pattern distinguishes log(1+x) from −log(1−x). Equal series values can come from different expansion points: simplify the logarithmic arguments before comparing the sums.',[('Sum Σₙ≥₁1/(n2ⁿ).','−log(1−1/2)=log2.'),('Sum Σₙ≥₁(−1)ⁿ⁺¹/(n2ⁿ).','log(1+1/2)=log(3/2).')])
add(30,'Real Analysis','Which set is countable?',['All polynomials with rational coefficients','All polynomials with real coefficients having rational roots','All 2×2 real matrices with rational eigenvalues','All real matrices whose row echelon form has rational entries'],'A',['For a fixed degree, rational coefficients form a finite Cartesian power of ℚ.','In each other choice, exhibit a family indexed injectively by arbitrary real numbers.'],'A=⋃ₙ≥₀ℚⁿ⁺¹ (with leading-zero identifications), so it is countable. B contains pₜ(x)=t(x−1) for every nonzero real t, all with rational root 1; this is uncountable. C contains [[0,t],[0,0]], t∈ℝ, all with eigenvalues 0. D contains [[t,0],[0,1]] for every t≠0: their reduced row echelon form is I with rational entries. Hence B,C,D are uncountable.','A set is countable if it is finite or can be listed by natural numbers. Finite Cartesian products of countable sets are countable, and countable unions of countable sets are countable. Polynomials have finite degree, so rational-coefficient polynomials fit this framework. A restriction on roots or eigenvalues need not restrict coefficients or entries to a countable set: scaling a polynomial and changing off-diagonal matrix entries can preserve the restricted data. To prove uncountability efficiently, inject an uncountable parameter set such as ℝ or ℝ∖{0} into the family. Distinct parameter values must produce distinct objects; simply mentioning real coefficients is not itself a proof.',[('Are finite binary strings countable?','Yes: strings of length n form a finite set of size 2ⁿ; union over n is countable.'),('Are monic linear polynomials with real coefficients countable?','No: x−t, t∈ℝ, is an injective uncountable family.')])
add(31,'Linear Algebra','A,B are n×n matrices. Every row of A sums to 2, and every column of B sums to 2. Which matrix is necessarily singular?',['I−(1/2)BAᵀ','I−(1/2)AB','I−(1/4)AB','I−(1/4)BAᵀ'],'D',['Let e=(1,…,1)ᵀ. The conditions give Ae=2e and eᵀB=2eᵀ.','Compute eᵀBAᵀ and obtain a nonzero left null vector for one choice.'],'eᵀBAᵀ=2eᵀAᵀ=2(Ae)ᵀ=4eᵀ, so eᵀ(I−BAᵀ/4)=0 and D is singular. A and B fail already for A=B=2I, when both proposed matrices are −I. To disprove C, take n=2, A=[[2,0],[2,0]], B=[[0,0],[2,2]]. These satisfy the sums, while AB=0 and I−AB/4=I is nonsingular.','Constant row sums give a right eigenvector consisting of ones, while constant column sums give a left eigenvector. Their orientation matters: a statement about Ae cannot be substituted for eᵀA. A square matrix is singular if it has either a nonzero right null vector or a nonzero left null vector. A product may inherit an eigenvalue when the same vector is an eigenvector for both relevant actions. Transposing one factor can align the orientations. Necessary claims must hold for every matrix satisfying the hypotheses, so counterexamples with small rank or diagonal matrices are often effective. AB and BA share characteristic polynomials for equal-sized square matrices, but BAᵀ is a different product.',[('Rows of A sum to 3. Prove A−3I is singular.','Ae=3e for e≠0, so (A−3I)e=0.'),('Columns of B sum to 5 and rows of A sum to 3. Give a guaranteed singular matrix.','I−BAᵀ/15: eᵀBAᵀ=15eᵀ.')])
add(32,'Linear Algebra','V={A∈M₃×₃(ℝ): Aᵀ+A∈ℝ·I}. For q(A)=(tr A)²−tr(A²), what is its signature?',['(+ + + +)','(+ 0 0 0)','(− − −)','(− − − 0)'],'A',['Write A=sI+K with K skew-symmetric.','A 3×3 skew-symmetric matrix has three independent off-diagonal entries, and tr(K²) is negative twice their squares.'],'Every A∈V uniquely equals sI+K, Kᵀ=−K. Writing K=[[0,a,b],[−a,0,c],[−b,−c,0]], tr A=3s and tr A²=3s²−2(a²+b²+c²). Hence q=6s²+2a²+2b²+2c². All four independent coordinates have positive coefficient, so the signature is (+ + + +), A. There are no zero or negative directions, excluding B,C,D.','A quadratic form on a finite-dimensional real vector space can be diagonalized by an invertible change of coordinates. Its signature records its positive, negative, and zero squared directions; these counts are invariant under such changes. Every real matrix decomposes uniquely into symmetric and skew-symmetric parts. The condition A+Aᵀ∈ℝI restricts the symmetric part to one scalar parameter. A skew-symmetric 3×3 matrix has three parameters and tr(K²)=−Σᵢⱼkᵢⱼ². Thus the minus sign before tr(A²) contributes positive skew directions. Trace expressions should be expanded before reading a signature, and the dimension of the allowed subspace must be counted.',[('On 2×2 matrices sI+[[0,a],[−a,0]], find (tr A)²−tr A².','tr A=2s, tr A²=2s²−2a², so q=2s²+2a², signature (++).'),('Find the signature of x²−2y²+0z².','One positive, one negative, one zero direction: (+−0).')])
add(33,'Complex Analysis','γ is the positively oriented circle |z|=3/2. If ∮γ eⁱᶻ/((z−1)(z−2i)²) dz=2πiC, find |C|.',['2','5','1/2','1/5'],'D',['Only the pole at z=1 lies inside the circle.','Its residue is eⁱ/(1−2i)²; take the modulus.'],'The pole z=1 has modulus 1<3/2; z=2i is outside. The residue theorem gives C=eⁱ/(1−2i)². Since |eⁱ|=1 and |1−2i|²=5, |C|=1/5, D. A,B,C do not equal this value. The order-two pole outside contributes no residue to this contour.','The residue theorem states that a positively oriented closed contour integral of a meromorphic function equals 2πi times the sum of residues at poles inside, provided none lie on the contour. At a simple pole a, the residue is lim(z−a)f(z). Before calculating, determine which singularities are enclosed; external poles do not contribute. The modulus of a complex quotient is the quotient of moduli, and |w²|=|w|². Also |eⁱᵗ|=1 for real t, whereas |eᶻ|=eᴿᵉᶻ in general. The contour orientation controls the sign but does not alter the modulus of the residue sum.',[('Evaluate ∮|z|=2 eᶻ/(z−1) dz, positive orientation.','Only pole 1 contributes residue e, so the integral is 2πie.'),('Find |Res(z=0) 1/[z(z−3i)]|.','Residue=1/(−3i), with modulus 1/3.')])
add(34,'Complex Analysis','D={z:|z|<1}, O(D) denotes holomorphic functions. A={f∈O(D): f(1/n)=e⁻ⁿ if n even, and 0 if n odd, n≥2}; B={f∈O(D): f(1/n)=(n−2)/(n−1), n≥2}. Which is true?',['Both nonempty','A empty and B has exactly one element','A has exactly one element and B empty','Both empty'],'B',['The odd reciprocal points are zeros accumulating at 0 inside D.','Rewrite (n−2)/(n−1) in terms of z=1/n to construct the function in B.'],'If f∈A, its zeros 1/(2k+1) accumulate at 0 in D. The identity theorem forces f≡0, contradicting its nonzero even-index values. Thus A is empty. For B, f(z)=(1−2z)/(1−z) is holomorphic in D and gives the required values. Any two functions in B agree on {1/n}, with interior limit point 0, so are equal by the identity theorem. Therefore B has exactly one element, giving choice B and rejecting A,C,D.','The identity theorem says that two holomorphic functions on a connected domain agreeing on a set with a limit point inside the domain agree everywhere. The zero-set version implies a nonzero holomorphic function has isolated zeros. The location of the accumulation point is crucial: convergence only to a boundary point does not trigger the theorem. Specified values on an interior-accumulating sequence determine at most one function, but existence must still be established. Rational interpolation formulas are useful when data depend algebraically on n and the nodes are 1/n. The candidate must have no poles inside the domain; a pole on the boundary is allowed for holomorphy in the open disc.',[('Can a nonzero holomorphic function on |z|<1 vanish at 1/n for every n≥2?','No: the zeros accumulate at 0 inside the disc, so the identity theorem forces the zero function.'),('Find holomorphic f on the disc with f(1/n)=n/(n+1).','f(z)=1/(1+z); its only pole is −1 on the boundary, and uniqueness follows from the identity theorem.')])
add(35,'Abstract Algebra','How many generators does a cyclic group of order 36 have?',['6','12','18','24'],'B',['gᵏ generates a cyclic group of order n exactly when gcd(k,n)=1.','Use φ(36)=36(1−1/2)(1−1/3).'],'If G=⟨g⟩ has order 36, ord(gᵏ)=36/gcd(k,36). Thus a generator corresponds to a unit residue modulo 36, and the number is φ(36)=36·(1/2)·(2/3)=12. B is correct; 6,18,24 count neither all nor only the residues coprime to 36.','In a cyclic group of order n, every element is a power gᵏ of a fixed generator. Its order is n/gcd(n,k). Therefore generators are precisely powers whose exponents are relatively prime to n. Euler’s totient φ(n) counts these residue classes. If n=∏pᵢᵃⁱ, then φ(n)=n∏(1−1/pᵢ), with the product over distinct prime divisors. Prime powers contribute pᵃ−pᵃ⁻¹ and the totient is multiplicative on coprime integers. Do not count all elements of large order as generators: their order must equal n exactly. The identity element is never a generator of a nontrivial cyclic group.',[('How many generators does a cyclic group of order 15 have?','φ(15)=15(1−1/3)(1−1/5)=8.'),('Find the order of g⁶ when ord(g)=36.','36/gcd(36,6)=6; g⁶ is not a generator.')],difficulty='Easy')
add(36,'Abstract Algebra','Which statement is necessarily true for a commutative ring R with unity?',['R may have no maximal ideals','R can have exactly two maximal ideals','R can have maximal ideals but no prime ideals','R has at least two prime ideals'],'B',['A nonzero commutative unital ring has a maximal ideal; every maximal ideal is prime.','For exactly two maximal ideals use a product of two fields or ℤ/6ℤ.'],'Under the usual convention 1≠0, every such ring has a maximal ideal, so A is false. Every maximal ideal is prime, ruling out C. A field has only the prime ideal (0), ruling out D. B is possible: ℤ/6ℤ has exactly the maximal ideals (2) and (3), since its quotient by either is a field and these correspond to the primes dividing 6. Thus B is the only valid assertion.','A proper ideal M of a commutative ring with identity is maximal if no proper ideal lies strictly between M and R. It is maximal exactly when R/M is a field. An ideal P is prime when ab∈P implies a∈P or b∈P; equivalently R/P is an integral domain. Fields are domains, so maximal ideals are prime. A nonzero unital ring has maximal ideals by Zorn’s lemma. Rings can have one, finitely many, or infinitely many maximal ideals. The standard hypothesis 1≠0 matters: the zero ring has no proper ideals. For ℤ/nℤ, maximal ideals correspond to distinct prime divisors of n.',[('How many maximal ideals does ℤ/12ℤ have?','Two, corresponding to primes 2 and 3: (2) and (3).'),('How many maximal ideals does a field F have?','Exactly one, (0), since it is the only proper ideal.')],note='Uses the conventional nonzero unital-ring assumption 1≠0; if the zero ring is admitted, the wording of A introduces an additional convention issue.')
add(37,'Complex Analysis','For nonconstant entire f(z)=u(x,y)+iv(x,y), which assertion is false?',['uₓ=vᵧ and uᵧ=−vₓ','uᵧ=vₓ and uₓ=−vᵧ','|f′(x+iy)|²=uₓ²+vₓ²','|f′(x+iy)|²=uᵧ²+vᵧ²'],'B',['Apply the Cauchy–Riemann equations.','If both the genuine equations and B held everywhere, both partial derivatives would vanish.'],'Holomorphy gives uₓ=vᵧ and uᵧ=−vₓ, so A is true. Also f′=uₓ+ivₓ=vᵧ−iuᵧ, proving C and D. B reverses both signs. If it held everywhere as well, uₓ=vᵧ=0 and uᵧ=vₓ=0, forcing f′≡0 and hence f constant, a contradiction. Thus B is false.','For a holomorphic function f=u+iv, the Cauchy–Riemann equations are uₓ=vᵧ and uᵧ=−vₓ. They follow by comparing derivatives along the real and imaginary directions. The complex derivative can consequently be written uₓ+ivₓ or vᵧ−iuᵧ. Taking squared modulus yields either sum of two squares in the question. Holomorphic functions are smooth, so these equations hold throughout their domain. A holomorphic function with derivative identically zero on a connected domain is constant. Cauchy–Riemann equations at one isolated point alone do not establish holomorphy; continuity of partial derivatives in a neighbourhood is a standard sufficient hypothesis.',[('For f(z)=z², verify Cauchy–Riemann.','u=x²−y²,v=2xy. uₓ=2x=vᵧ, and uᵧ=−2y=−vₓ.'),('Compute |f′(z)|² for f(z)=z² using partial derivatives.','uₓ²+vₓ²=4x²+4y²=4|z|².')])
add(38,'Number Theory','S={n:1≤n≤999, 3 divides n or 37 divides n}. Find the cardinality of its complement in {1,…,999}.',['639','648','666','990'],'B',['Use inclusion–exclusion for multiples of 3 and 37.','The overlap consists of multiples of 111.'],'There are ⌊999/3⌋=333 multiples of 3, ⌊999/37⌋=27 multiples of 37, and ⌊999/111⌋=9 common multiples. Hence |S|=333+27−9=351 and |Sᶜ|=999−351=648, B. The remaining choices do not equal the inclusion–exclusion count.','Among the integers 1 through N, the number divisible by d is ⌊N/d⌋. The integers divisible by both a and b are divisible by lcm(a,b), which equals ab only when a and b are coprime. Inclusion–exclusion gives the size of a union as the sum of the sizes minus their intersection. Complement counting subtracts this union from N. Endpoint conventions matter: the interval here includes both 1 and 999. A frequent mistake is to subtract both multiple counts without adding back their overlap, thereby excluding common multiples twice. For more divisibility conditions, inclusion–exclusion continues with alternating pairwise, triple, and higher intersections.',[('Count integers in 1,…,100 divisible by neither 2 nor 5.','100−50−20+10=40.'),('Count integers ≤60 divisible by 4 or 6.','15+10−5=20, since lcm(4,6)=12.')],difficulty='Easy')
add(39,'Complex Analysis','f(z)=(z³+2z−4)/z. What is the radius of convergence of its Taylor series at z=1?',['0','1','2','∞'],'B',['Simplify f(z)=z²+2−4/z.','The nearest genuine singularity to the expansion centre is z=0.'],'f(z)=z²+2−4/z has a genuine simple pole at 0, since the numerator there is −4. Its distance from centre 1 is 1, giving radius 1, B. Explicitly 1/z=1/(1+(z−1))=Σₙ≥₀(−1)ⁿ(z−1)ⁿ for |z−1|<1. The polynomial part changes only finitely many coefficients and cannot remove the pole. Thus A,C,D are false.','A holomorphic function has a Taylor series around an interior point, converging in the largest disc centred there on which its analytic continuation is holomorphic. For a rational function the radius is the distance to its nearest nonremovable pole after cancelling common factors. Zeros of a denominator that cancel with the numerator are removable and do not bound the radius for the extended function. A polynomial has infinite Taylor radius. The expansion centre need not be the origin, so distances must be measured from the given centre. A singularity lying on the boundary of the maximal disc determines the radius even when the function is well defined at many other points outside that disc.',[('Find the Taylor radius of 1/(z−3) at z=1.','The pole at 3 is distance 2 away, so R=2.'),('Find the Taylor radius at 0 of (z−2)/(z²−4) after extension.','It simplifies to 1/(z+2), whose nearest pole is −2; R=2.')])
add(40,'Topology','On a metric space (X,d), f:X→X satisfies d(f(x),f(y))≤d(x,y) for all x,y. Which is necessarily true?',['f is continuous','f is injective','f is surjective','f is injective if and only if it is surjective'],'A',['The hypothesis is a Lipschitz bound with constant 1.','A constant map disproves injectivity and surjectivity; x↦x/2 on [0,1] tests the equivalence.'],'For ε>0 take δ=ε: d(x,y)<δ implies d(f(x),f(y))≤d(x,y)<ε. Thus f is continuous, A. A constant map on a two-point metric space satisfies the hypothesis but is neither injective nor surjective, disproving B and C. For D, f(x)=x/2 on [0,1] is injective and satisfies the bound, but is not onto [0,1].','A map between metric spaces is Lipschitz with constant L if output distances are bounded by L times input distances. Such maps are uniformly continuous; for L>0 one may choose δ=ε/L. A nonexpansive map has L=1, as in this question. A contraction has a constant strictly below one and is a stronger property. Distance upper bounds do not ensure injectivity, since distinct points may be collapsed, or surjectivity, since the image may be smaller than the target. Injectivity would follow from a positive lower distance bound, and fixed-point conclusions for contractions require additional conditions such as completeness. Always distinguish a map’s codomain from its actual image.',[('Prove x↦sin x is uniformly continuous on ℝ.','The mean-value theorem gives |sin x−sin y|≤|x−y|, a Lipschitz bound.'),('Give a nonexpansive self-map of ℝ that is injective but not onto.','f(x)=arctan x is injective, derivative≤1, and image (−π/2,π/2) is a proper subset of ℝ.')])
add(41,'Ordinary Differential Equations','If y solves 4xy″+2y′+y=0 with y(0)=1, find y″(0).',['1/24','1/12','1/6','1/2'],'B',['Evaluate the differential equation at x=0 to find y′(0).','Differentiate the equation once: 4xy‴+6y″+y′=0, then set x=0.'],'At x=0, 2y′(0)+y(0)=0, so y′(0)=−1/2. Differentiating gives 4xy‴+6y″+y′=0; at zero, 6y″(0)=1/2 and y″(0)=1/12, B. The other fractions fail this equation. Equivalently a power series y=Σaₙxⁿ gives a₁=−1/2 and a₂=1/24, so y″(0)=2a₂=1/12; this also explains why A is a tempting coefficient/derivative confusion.','At an ordinary point, a second-order linear ODE is solved for y″ by dividing by its leading coefficient. Here that coefficient vanishes at zero, so the point is singular and direct substitution instead imposes a compatibility condition on y′(0). For a smooth solution, differentiation can yield further compatibility relations. Power-series substitution is another route: match coefficients of equal powers and remember y⁽ⁿ⁾(0)=n!aₙ, not aₙ. Singular-point initial data cannot always be chosen independently. The finite solution selected by y(0)=1 has a regular series, and the recurrence determines its derivatives; a singular branch would not satisfy the stated finite initial condition.',[('Find y′(0) if 2xy″+y′+y=0 and y(0)=3.','Substitution at zero gives y′(0)+3=0, so y′(0)=−3.'),('If y(x)=1−x/2+x²/24+⋯, find y″(0).','Differentiate twice or multiply the x² coefficient by 2!: y″(0)=1/12.')])
add(42,'Classical Mechanics','A body moves freely in a uniform gravitational field. Which assertion is true?',['Stable equilibrium is possible','Stable equilibrium is not possible','Stable equilibrium depends on field strength','Equilibrium is metastable'],'B',['A free body experiences the same nonzero gravitational force everywhere.','An equilibrium requires zero net force; the linear gravitational potential has no minimum.'],'For a nonzero uniform gravitational field g, the free body obeys r̈=g and has force mg≠0 at every position. Therefore no stationary equilibrium is possible, and in particular stable equilibrium is impossible: B. A is false, C cannot restore an equilibrium by changing a nonzero constant strength, and D requires an equilibrium that is absent. If the field were zero, free translational perturbations still do not produce a restoring force.','Mechanical equilibrium requires zero net force. For conservative systems the force is −∇V, so equilibrium is a stationary point of the potential, and a strict local minimum generally gives stable equilibrium for a particle. A uniform gravitational field has a potential linear in position, V=−mg·r plus a constant. Its gradient is constant and nonzero, with no stationary point or minimum. Constraints or supporting forces can produce equilibria, but they are excluded by the word freely. Stable equilibrium means sufficiently small disturbances remain nearby; metastability requires a local trapping configuration separated from lower-energy states. Neither follows from a constant-force potential.',[('Can a free particle be at equilibrium in a nonzero uniform electric field?','No: force qE is constant and nonzero when q≠0.'),('Is x=0 stable for V(x)=kx²/2 with k>0?','Yes: it is a strict potential minimum and the restoring force is −kx.')],difficulty='Easy')
add(43,'Calculus of Variations','Find an extremal of J(y)=∫₋₁¹[(y′)²−2xy]dx with y(−1)=−1 and y(1)=1.',['−x³/5+6x/5','−x⁵/8+9x/8','−x³/6+7x/6','−x³/7+8x/7'],'C',['Use Fᵧ−d(Fᵧ′)/dx=0.','This yields y″=−x; integrate and impose both endpoint values.'],'F=(y′)²−2xy gives Fᵧ=−2x,Fᵧ′=2y′, hence −2x−2y″=0. Integrating y″=−x gives y=−x³/6+C₁x+C₂. The endpoints yield C₂=0 and C₁=7/6, so C. Each listed curve has the endpoints, but A has y″=−6x/5, B has y″=−(5/2)x³, and D has y″=−6x/7; only C satisfies the Euler–Lagrange equation.','For a functional ∫F(x,y,y′)dx with fixed endpoint values, a smooth extremal satisfies Fᵧ−d(Fᵧ′)/dx=0. This follows by differentiating the functional along y+εη, where η vanishes at both endpoints, and integrating by parts. The endpoint conditions determine the constants of the resulting ODE. Satisfying the endpoint values alone is insufficient. Here the derivative term is quadratic and strictly convex, while the y term is linear. The second variation is 2∫(η′)²dx>0 for nonzero admissible η, so the computed extremal is also the unique minimizer. When checking options, a second derivative often eliminates choices more quickly than substituting full formulas.',[('Minimize ∫₀¹(y′)²dx with y(0)=0,y(1)=2.','Euler–Lagrange gives y″=0, so y=2x.'),('Find an extremal of ∫₀¹[(y′)²−2y]dx with zero endpoint values.','y″=−1, hence y=−x²/2+x/2 after imposing y(0)=y(1)=0.')])
add(44,'Numerical Analysis','The rule ∫₋₁¹f(x)dx=a f(−1)+b f′(0)+c f′(1) is exact for all polynomials of degree ≤2. Find a+b+c.',['4','3','2','1'],'A',['Apply the rule to 1,x,x².','The equations are a=2, −a+b+c=0, and a+2c=2/3.'],'For f=1, a=2. For f=x, 0=−a+b+c, so b+c=2. For f=x², 2/3=a+2c, hence c=−2/3 and b=8/3. Thus a+b+c=2+8/3−2/3=4, A, rejecting 3,2,1. Derivative evaluations vanish on constants but not on x, so treating this as an ordinary three-point rule would give incorrect equations.','A linear quadrature rule is exact on all polynomials of degree at most m if and only if it is exact on the monomial basis 1,x,…,xᵐ. This reduces exactness to a finite system of moment equations. Rules may use derivative values as well as function values; such Hermite-type rules must evaluate those derivatives separately for each monomial. Symmetry of the integration interval makes odd moments zero. Exactness coefficients need not be positive, especially for derivative terms. The degree of exactness concerns the whole polynomial space, rather than a few convenient functions without a basis argument. Always retain the integration limits when calculating moments.',[('Find a,b for ∫₀¹f≈af(0)+bf(1), exact through degree 1.','For 1: a+b=1. For x: b=1/2. Thus a=b=1/2.'),('Find c in ∫₋₁¹f≈2f(0)+cf″(0), exact through degree 2.','For x²: 2/3=2c, so c=1/3; constants and x are already exact.')])
add(45,'Ordinary Differential Equations','Consider (A) x′=x³, x(0)=1; (B) x′=x sin(x²), x(0)=2. I: A blows up in finite time. II: B blows up in finite time. Which is true?',['Both true','I true, II false','Both false','I false, II true'],'B',['Solve A by separation.','For B use |x′|≤|x| and an exponential bound, or inspect the adjacent zeros of sin(x²).'],'A: x⁻²=1−2t, so x(t)=(1−2t)⁻¹ᐟ² blows up at t=1/2. B: |x′|=|x sin(x²)|≤|x|, hence |x(t)|≤2eᵗ for finite t≥0 by Grönwall. Its smooth right-hand side gives local uniqueness and extension while bounded, so finite-time blow-up is impossible. More specifically 2 lies between √π and √(2π), and x′<0 there; the solution tends toward √π. Thus I true and II false, B; A,C,D have incorrect truth combinations.','Finite-time blow-up means a solution becomes unbounded as time approaches a finite endpoint of its maximal existence interval. Superlinear growth can cause it, as x′=x³ demonstrates. Smoothness of the right-hand side provides local existence and uniqueness but does not guarantee global solutions. A linear growth bound |f(x)|≤C(1+|x|) prevents finite-time blow-up through Grönwall’s inequality and the continuation theorem. Bounded factors such as sine can reduce a seemingly nonlinear expression to linear growth. Equilibrium barriers also constrain one-dimensional autonomous solutions: uniqueness prevents crossing an equilibrium in finite time. Distinguish unbounded growth as t→∞ from a singularity at finite time.',[('Solve x′=x²,x(0)=2 and find its blow-up time.','x=2/(1−2t), blowing up at t=1/2.'),('Can x′=x cos x blow up in finite forward time?','No: |x′|≤|x| gives |x(t)|≤|x(0)|eᵗ.')])
add(46,'Partial Differential Equations','Which PDE is NOT parabolic for all x,y∈ℝ?',['x²uₓᵧ−2xyuᵧ+y²=0','x²uₓₓ−2xyuₓᵧ+y²uᵧᵧ=0','x²uₓₓ+2xyuₓᵧ+y²uᵧᵧ=0','x²uₓₓ−2xyuₓᵧ+y²uᵧᵧ+xuₓ+yuᵧ=0'],'A',['Only the coefficients of second derivatives enter the discriminant.','In Auₓₓ+2Buₓᵧ+Cuᵧᵧ, compare B²−AC for the four choices.'],'For A, principal coefficients are A₀=0,B₀=x²/2,C₀=0, so B₀²−A₀C₀=x⁴/4, positive whenever x≠0; it is hyperbolic there and thus not parabolic everywhere. B,D have coefficients x²,−xy,y², with discriminant zero. C has x²,xy,y², again zero. Lower-order terms in A,D do not affect classification. Hence the intended discriminant-based answer is A. At the origin all principal coefficients in B,C,D vanish; these are degenerate points rather than strictly second-order parabolic operators.','The principal part of a real second-order PDE in two variables is Auₓₓ+2Buₓᵧ+Cuᵧᵧ. The discriminant B²−AC classifies nondegenerate points as elliptic when negative, hyperbolic when positive, and parabolic when zero with a nonzero rank-one principal matrix. If the mixed term is written Buₓᵧ instead, the equivalent discriminant is B²−4AC. Lower-order derivative and forcing terms are irrelevant to this classification. Variable coefficients can change the type across the domain, and points where every second-order coefficient vanishes must be described separately as degenerate. The paper uses the algebraic zero-discriminant convention for the alternatives containing x² and y².',[('Classify uₓₓ+2uₓᵧ+uᵧᵧ=0.','A=C=1,B=1 gives discriminant 0 and nonzero rank-one principal matrix: parabolic.'),('Classify uₓᵧ+u=0.','A=C=0,B=1/2 gives discriminant 1/4>0: hyperbolic.')],note='A is the intended discriminant answer. B,C,D have vanishing principal part at (0,0); the literal phrase “for all x,y” ignores this standard degeneracy.')
add(47,'Partial Differential Equations','Solve uᵧ−xuₓ+u−1=0 for y≥0 with u(x,0)=sin x. Find u(0,1).',['1−1/e','1+1/e','1−(1−sin e)/e','1+(1−sin e)/e'],'A',['Characteristics satisfy dx/dy=−x. At x=0 the characteristic remains x=0.','Along it u′+u=1 and u(0)=sin0=0.'],'The characteristic through (0,1) is x(y)=0. Along it du/dy=1−u with initial u=0, so u=1−e⁻ʸ and u(0,1)=1−1/e, A. In general x(y)=ξe⁻ʸ, hence u(x,y)=1+e⁻ʸ(sin(xeʸ)−1), which confirms the value. B,C,D have additional nonzero terms and fail.','For a first-order linear PDE a uₓ+b uᵧ+c u=d, characteristics solve x′=a,y′=b. Along these curves the PDE becomes the scalar ODE u′+cu=d. Initial values are transported from the specified initial curve, which should be noncharacteristic for local uniqueness. When y itself parametrizes the characteristics, dx/dy=a/b is convenient. The position at the initial line must be recovered by tracing the characteristic backward, rather than holding x fixed unless it actually stays fixed. Here x=0 is such an invariant characteristic, making the requested value much easier than solving everywhere. An integrating factor handles the remaining linear ODE.',[('Solve uᵧ+u=1,u(x,0)=x.','x is constant along characteristics; u=1+(x−1)e⁻ʸ.'),('Solve uᵧ−xuₓ=0,u(x,0)=x².','Characteristic label ξ=xeʸ, so u=x²e²ʸ.')])
add(48,'Numerical Analysis','For y′=y−t²+1, 0≤t≤2, y(0)=0.5, Euler’s method uses h=0.4. Which listed bound applies to |y(0.8)−w(0.8)|?',['0.2(0.5e²−2)(e⁰·⁴−1)','0.1(e⁰·⁴−1)','0.2(0.5e²−2)(e⁰·⁸−1)','0.1(e⁰·⁸−1)'],'C',['The standard Euler bound is hM(eᴸᵗ−1)/(2L), where M bounds |y″| over the given interval.','Here L=1, y=(t+1)²−0.5eᵗ, and M=0.5e²−2 on [0,2].'],'Solving the IVP gives y=(t+1)²−0.5eᵗ, so y″=2−0.5eᵗ. Over [0,2], M=max|y″|=max(1.5,0.5e²−2)=0.5e²−2. Since |∂f/∂y|=1, L=1. The Euler global bound at t=0.8 is (0.4M/2)(e⁰·⁸−1)=0.2(0.5e²−2)(e⁰·⁸−1), C. A uses the wrong elapsed time; B,D use an unsupported M=0.5. As a check, Euler gives w₁=1.1,w₂=1.876, while y(0.8)=3.24−0.5e⁰·⁸≈2.12723, with actual error≈0.25123 below the stated bound.','Forward Euler uses wₙ₊₁=wₙ+h f(tₙ,wₙ). Taylor’s theorem bounds each local truncation error by Mh²/2 if |y″|≤M. A Lipschitz bound L for f in its solution variable controls accumulation, yielding |y(tₙ)−wₙ|≤hM(eᴸ⁽ᵗⁿ⁻ᵗ⁰⁾−1)/(2L), when the initial error is zero. M must be a valid bound over the interval under consideration, and the exponent uses elapsed time, not step size. One may derive a sharper bound on a shorter interval, but the supplied expression uses the full specified interval [0,2]. The method has global error of order h and local error of order h².',[('Euler for y′=y,y(0)=1,h=0.1: find w(0.2).','w₁=1.1,w₂=1.21.'),('With L=2,M=6,h=0.1,t=1, what standard Euler bound follows?','hM(e²−1)/(2L)=0.15(e²−1).')])
add(49,'Statistical Inference','X|λ∼Poisson(λ), λ>0, with an exponential prior of mean 1/4. Observing X=0, which is the shortest 95% Bayesian credible interval for λ?',['(0,c), c=0.95b [as printed]','(0,c), c=log(20)/5','(c,exp c), c=log(20)/5','(0.2−c,0.2+c), c=log(20)/5'],'B',['The likelihood for zero is e⁻λ; multiply it by the prior density 4e⁻⁴λ.','The posterior is exponential with rate 5, decreasing on its support, so the shortest interval starts at zero.'],'The posterior density is 5e⁻⁵λ for λ>0. For a decreasing density, any fixed-length interval has its largest probability when placed at the left support endpoint, so the shortest 95% interval is (0,c). Solve 1−e⁻⁵ᶜ=0.95: c=log20/5, B. A has an undefined printed symbol and does not specify this endpoint. C starts above zero and cannot be shortest; D extends below the support and wastes interval length.','A Bayesian posterior is proportional to likelihood times prior. A Gamma(shape α,rate β) prior is conjugate for Poisson observations: one count x updates it to Gamma(α+x,β+1). An exponential prior is Gamma(1,β), so a zero count preserves shape one and increases the rate. A credible interval contains a specified posterior probability, unlike a repeated-sampling confidence interval. The shortest interval follows the highest-density region. For a monotone decreasing posterior on (0,∞), this region starts at zero. Parameterization matters: exponential mean θ corresponds to rate 1/θ, and logarithmic endpoints arise from its survival probability.',[('Poisson count 0 with Exp(rate 2) prior: find a 90% shortest credible interval.','Posterior Exp(rate 3), so (0,log10/3).'),('Poisson count 3 with Gamma(2,rate 4) prior: identify posterior.','Gamma(shape 5,rate 5), from multiplying λ³e⁻λ by λe⁻⁴λ.')],track='Statistics')
add(50,'Probability','A proportion p of a large population has a disease. Blood from k independently sampled people is pooled and tests positive. What is the probability exactly one sampled person has the disease?',['kp(1−p)ᵏ⁻¹/[1−(1−p)ᵏ]','[(1−p)ᵏ+kp(1−p)ᵏ⁻¹]/[1−(1−p)ᵏ]','p/(p+p²+⋯+pᵏ)','1/k'],'A',['Let N be the number infected; N∼Binomial(k,p).','Condition on N≥1, since a positive accurate pooled test rules out N=0.'],'P(N=1)=kp(1−p)ᵏ⁻¹ and P(N≥1)=1−(1−p)ᵏ. Because {N=1}⊂{N≥1}, the conditional probability is their quotient, A. B incorrectly includes N=0 in the numerator after conditioning it out. C lacks binomial factors and D ignores p; e.g. as p→1 with k>1 the desired probability→0, while D remains 1/k.','Conditional probability is P(E|F)=P(E∩F)/P(F), provided P(F)>0. For k independent Bernoulli trials with success probability p, their count has Binomial(k,p) mass C(k,j)pʲ(1−p)ᵏ⁻ʲ. Conditioning on at least one success produces a zero-truncated binomial distribution: each positive-count mass is divided by 1−(1−p)ᵏ. The factor k for exactly one success chooses which participant is infected. A pooled positive result gives information about the total count but does not identify a participant. Independence is justified by sampling from a large population; finite sampling without replacement would instead lead to a hypergeometric count.',[('Two independent disease indicators have p=0.2. Given at least one, find probability exactly one.','2·0.2·0.8/(1−0.8²)=0.32/0.36=8/9.'),('For k=3,p=1/2, find P(N=2|N≥1).','(3/8)/(7/8)=3/7.')],track='Statistics')
add(51,'Stochastic Processes','A chain on {1,2,3,4,5} has P=[[0,.3,.7,0,0],[0,0,.3,.7,0],[0,0,0,.3,.7],[.7,0,0,0,.3],[.3,.7,0,0,0]]. Which is always true?',['State 1 has period 2','State 2 is recurrent','State 3 is transient','At least two stationary distributions exist'],'B',['The positive .3 transitions cycle 1→2→3→4→5→1, so all states communicate.','A finite irreducible chain is positive recurrent and has a unique stationary distribution. Check return lengths 3 and 5 for the period.'],'The .3 edges give a directed five-cycle, proving irreducibility. Every state in a finite irreducible chain is positive recurrent, so B is true and C false. There is exactly one stationary distribution, ruling out D; here the matrix is doubly stochastic and the stationary distribution is uniform. State 1 can return in 3 steps via 1→2→4→1 and in 5 via the five-cycle; gcd(3,5)=1, so its period is 1, not 2, ruling out A.','States communicate when each is reachable from the other with positive probability in finitely many steps. A chain is irreducible if all states communicate. Every finite irreducible chain is positive recurrent and has a unique stationary probability vector π satisfying πP=π. Period is the greatest common divisor of possible return times, and all states in an irreducible class share it. A chain can have no self-loops yet still be aperiodic when return cycles have coprime lengths. Doubly stochastic finite matrices have uniform stationary distribution, but uniqueness still requires irreducibility. Recurrence concerns eventual return probability, rather than the probability of a return in one specified step.',[('A deterministic cycle of length 4 has what period?','Return times are multiples of 4; every state has period 4.'),('A finite irreducible chain has a positive self-loop at one state. What is its period?','That state has return time 1, hence period 1, shared by all states.')],track='Statistics')
add(52,'Sampling and Design of Experiments','A BIBD has v treatments, b blocks, k plots per block, r occurrences per treatment and λ occurrences per treatment pair. Which identity necessarily holds?',['vb=rk','vr=bk','r(b−1)=λ(k−1)','r(v−1)=λ(b−1)'],'B',['Count treatment–block incidences in two ways.','Counting by treatments gives vr; counting by blocks gives bk.'],'There are v treatments each occurring r times, hence vr incidences; b blocks each contain k plots, hence bk incidences. Thus vr=bk, B. The other standard identity is r(k−1)=λ(v−1), obtained by fixing a treatment and counting its partners. A,C,D exchange parameters incorrectly. For example the Fano BIBD (v,b,r,k,λ)=(7,7,3,3,1) satisfies B but violates A,C,D.','A balanced incomplete block design has v treatments arranged in b blocks of equal size k<v. Every treatment occurs in r blocks, and every distinct pair occurs together in λ blocks. Counting all treatment incidences gives vr=bk. Fixing a treatment and counting its co-occurrences gives r(k−1)=λ(v−1): its r blocks each supply k−1 partners, while each of the other v−1 treatments pairs with it λ times. These identities impose necessary integer constraints on possible designs, but their satisfaction alone does not guarantee existence. The meanings of v,b,r,k,λ should be kept distinct: r is replication per treatment and λ is replication per pair.',[('A BIBD has v=9,k=3,r=4. Find b.','b=vr/k=9·4/3=12.'),('For v=9,k=3,r=4, find λ.','λ=r(k−1)/(v−1)=4·2/8=1.')],track='Statistics',difficulty='Easy')
add(53,'Statistical Inference','X₁,…,X₁₆ are iid normal with mean μ and variance 4. For the most powerful test H₀:μ=3 against H₁:μ=0, the observed mean is 2.5. With Z∼N(0,1), what is the p-value?',['P(Z>1)','P(Z>−1)','P(Z>0.5)','P(Z>−0.5)'],'A',['The alternative is a smaller mean, so reject for small sample means.','Under H₀ the standard error is 2/√16=0.5, giving observed z=(2.5−3)/0.5=−1.'],'Neyman–Pearson gives a lower-tail test. Under H₀, X̄∼N(3,4/16). Thus p=P₀(X̄≤2.5)=P(Z≤−1)=P(Z≥1) by symmetry, A. B is the opposite tail; C,D use the wrong standardized magnitude. The value is about 0.1587.','For two simple hypotheses, the Neyman–Pearson lemma yields the most powerful test by thresholding the likelihood ratio. In a normal mean problem with known common variance, the ratio is monotone in the sample mean. A smaller alternative mean leads to rejection for small means, so the p-value is the null lower-tail probability at the observation. The sample mean variance is σ²/n, and its standard error is σ/√n. Standardization uses the null mean, not the alternative mean. Symmetry of the standard normal transforms P(Z≤−a) into P(Z≥a). A p-value quantifies extremeness under the null and is not the posterior probability that the null is true.',[('n=25,σ=5,H₀:μ=10 vs μ<10, observed X̄=8. Find p.','SE=1,z=−2; p=Φ(−2)=P(Z>2).'),('Same setup but alternative μ>10, observed X̄=12. Find p.','z=2; use upper tail p=1−Φ(2).')],track='Statistics')
add(54,'Probability','X₁,X₂,… are iid Uniform[0,1], and Yₙ,ₖ is the kth order statistic among the first n. Find P(Y₂₁,₇=Y₂₂,₇).',['1/3','2/3','7/11','15/22'],'D',['The extra observation preserves the seventh order statistic exactly when it lies above that statistic.','Its rank among all 22 iid continuous observations is uniform on {1,…,22}.'],'Ties have probability zero. If X₂₂ has rank at most 7 in the combined sample, the seventh statistic changes; if it has rank 8 through 22, the previous seventh remains seventh. The 22 ranks are equally likely by exchangeability, giving 15/22, D. A,B,C are different fractions. An alternative is E[1−Y₂₁,₇]=1−7/22=15/22.','Order statistics are observations sorted increasingly. For iid continuous variables, ties have probability zero and each observation has a uniformly distributed rank. When one new observation is added to an n-sample, the kth order statistic is unchanged exactly when the new observation is ranked above k in the n+1 combined observations. This yields probability (n+1−k)/(n+1), independently of the particular continuous distribution. For a uniform sample, Yₙ,ₖ has Beta(k,n+1−k) distribution and mean k/(n+1), giving another conditional expectation proof. Equality of order statistics from overlapping samples can have positive probability even though individual observations have continuous distributions, because the same observation may occupy both positions.',[('Adding a sixth observation to five iid continuous observations, what is the chance the minimum stays the same?','New rank must be 2,…,6, so probability 5/6.'),('Adding one observation to an n-sample, chance the maximum stays the same?','New observation must not be the largest: n/(n+1).')],track='Statistics')
add(55,'Statistical Inference','X₁,…,X₄ are iid Uniform(0,θ), θ>0. Let M=max Xᵢ. Compare coverage of I=[2M,3M] and J=[M,1+M] for θ.',['Both independent of θ','I independent of θ; J not independent of θ','J independent of θ; I not independent of θ','Neither independent of θ'],'B',['M/θ has cdf t⁴ on [0,1].','Rewrite θ∈I as 1/3≤M/θ≤1/2; rewrite θ∈J as M≥θ−1.'],'Pθ(θ∈I)=P(θ/3≤M≤θ/2)=(1/2)⁴−(1/3)⁴=1/16−1/81, independent of θ. For J, M≤θ always and θ≤M+1 requires M≥θ−1. Coverage is 1 when 0<θ≤1 and 1−((θ−1)/θ)⁴ when θ>1, depending on θ. Therefore B is true; A,C,D have incorrect dependence statements.','For a Uniform(0,θ) sample of size n, the maximum M has cdf Pθ(M≤m)=(m/θ)ⁿ for 0≤m≤θ. Consequently M/θ is a pivot: its distribution does not depend on θ. Confidence intervals formed by multiplying M by fixed constants translate into fixed pivot bounds, giving parameter-independent coverage. Additive shifts such as M+1 introduce an absolute scale and usually destroy that invariance. Coverage is the probability over repeated samples that the random interval contains the fixed true parameter. It need not be a desired nominal level unless constants were chosen accordingly. Support restrictions must be accounted for when θ−1 is negative.',[('n=2, I=[M,2M]. Find coverage.','θ∈I iff M≥θ/2; coverage=1−(1/2)²=3/4.'),('n=3, J=[M,M+2]. Find coverage for θ>2.','1−((θ−2)/θ)³; for θ≤2 coverage is 1.')],track='Statistics')
add(56,'Statistical Inference','X₁,…,Xₙ are iid exponential with mean θ. Which statistic is NOT sufficient for θ?',['1/(X₁+⋯+Xₙ)','X₁+⋯+Xₙ','Xₙ/(X₁+⋯+Xₙ₋₁)','(Xₙ,X₁+⋯+Xₙ₋₁)'],'C',['The likelihood depends on data through S=ΣXᵢ.','A one-to-one transformation of S remains sufficient, and a vector that determines S is sufficient; the scale-free ratio loses S.'],'The joint density is θ⁻ⁿexp(−S/θ) on the positive orthant, so S is sufficient (B). 1/S determines S and is also sufficient (A). The vector in D determines S as the sum of its components, so is sufficient. C is invariant under multiplying all observations by any positive constant and cannot determine S. More formally it is ancillary: writing Xᵢ=θZᵢ makes the ratio distribution independent of θ, while the conditional distribution of S still depends on θ. Thus C is not sufficient.','A statistic T is sufficient when the conditional distribution of the full data given T is free of the parameter. The factorization theorem establishes sufficiency by writing the density as gθ(T(x))h(x). In a regular exponential family, the natural sum often forms a sufficient statistic. Any invertible transformation of a sufficient statistic is sufficient, and retaining additional information preserves sufficiency. Conversely, a coarser statistic can lose information. In an exponential scale model, normalized ratios are ancillary, meaning their marginal distribution is parameter-free; this is distinct from sufficiency. The likelihood-ratio criterion shows that data sharing a sufficient value must have parameter-independent likelihood ratio.',[('Are the mean and sum both sufficient for an exponential mean with fixed n?','Yes: X̄=S/n is a one-to-one transformation of S.'),('For n=2, compare data (1,1) and (2,2) with statistic X₂/X₁.','Both ratios equal 1, but likelihood ratio exp(2/θ) depends on θ; the ratio is not sufficient.')],track='Statistics')
add(57,'Stochastic Processes','For an M/M/1 queue with traffic intensity ρ<1, find the steady-state probability of n customers in the system.',['ρⁿ','ρ(1−ρⁿ)','ρⁿ⁻¹(1−ρ)','ρⁿ(1−ρ)'],'D',['Stationary birth–death balance gives πₙ₊₁=ρπₙ.','Normalize Σₙ≥₀πₙ=1 using the geometric series.'],'With arrival rate λ and service rate μ, ρ=λ/μ<1. Balance λπₙ=μπₙ₊₁ gives πₙ=π₀ρⁿ. Summing over n≥0 yields π₀/(1−ρ)=1, so πₙ=(1−ρ)ρⁿ, D. A omits normalization, B is not a mass sequence, and C uses the support convention n≥1 instead of the customer count beginning at zero.','An M/M/1 queue has Poisson arrivals, exponential independent service times, and one server. Its customer-count process is a birth–death chain with birth rate λ at every state and death rate μ whenever the state is positive. Stability requires ρ=λ/μ<1; otherwise no normalizable stationary distribution exists. Local balance produces a geometric ratio between adjacent stationary masses. The zero-customer probability is 1−ρ, also the long-run idle probability of the server. Counts in the system include a customer in service and take values 0,1,2,… . A geometric distribution can be parameterized on either this support or {1,2,…}, so exponent conventions must match the count being asked.',[('ρ=0.8: find the probability the system is empty.','π₀=1−ρ=0.2.'),('ρ=1/2: find P(N≥3) in stationarity.','Σₙ≥₃(1−ρ)ρⁿ=ρ³=1/8.')],track='Statistics',difficulty='Easy')
add(58,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis','For n>2 paired observations with all xᵢ distinct and all yᵢ distinct, Pearson rₚ=1. What must Spearman rₛ be?',['0.5<rₛ<1','rₛ=0.5','rₛ=1','rₛ=−1'],'C',['Equality in Cauchy–Schwarz implies a positive affine relation between centered observations.','A positive affine transformation preserves every rank.'],'rₚ=1 implies yᵢ−ȳ=c(xᵢ−x̄) for some c>0, by equality in Cauchy–Schwarz. Hence yᵢ=a+cxᵢ and the rank ordering of y exactly matches that of x. With no ties, rank differences are all zero, so rₛ=1−6Σdᵢ²/[n(n²−1)]=1, C. A excludes the endpoint, B differs, and D would require reversed ranks.','Pearson correlation measures linear association by the normalized centered inner product. Its magnitude is at most one by Cauchy–Schwarz, with equality exactly when centered data vectors are proportional. Positive correlation one means a positive slope, while negative one means a negative slope. Spearman correlation is the Pearson correlation of ranks and measures monotone association. With no ties it can be written 1−6Σdᵢ²/[n(n²−1)]. A strictly increasing transformation preserves ranks, so perfect positive linear relation implies perfect positive rank relation. The converse does not hold: ranks can match perfectly under a nonlinear increasing relation, with Pearson correlation less than one.',[('x=(1,2,3),y=(2,4,6). Find both correlations.','Both are 1 because y=2x and ranks agree.'),('x=(1,2,3),y=(1,4,9). Find Spearman correlation.','Ranks agree, so rₛ=1; Pearson is below 1 because the points are not on a line.')],track='Statistics')
add(59,'Statistical Inference','Which nonparametric test is appropriate for checking whether generated observations follow Uniform(0,1)?',['Wilcoxon signed rank test','Sign test','Paired t test','Kolmogorov–Smirnov test'],'D',['This is a one-sample goodness-of-fit question for a specified continuous cdf.','Compare the empirical cdf with F₀(x)=x on (0,1).'],'The one-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov statistic Dₙ=supₓ|Fₙ(x)−F₀(x)| compares the empirical distribution with the specified uniform cdf, so D. Wilcoxon and sign tests chiefly assess location/symmetry features, rather than the whole uniform distribution; paired t is parametric and tests a mean difference. The KS test checks the marginal distribution under an iid null; serial independence of a generator requires additional dependence checks.','Goodness-of-fit testing asks whether a sample distribution matches a specified model. For continuous fully specified F₀, the one-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov statistic is the maximum vertical gap between the empirical cdf and F₀. Under the iid null its distribution is independent of the particular continuous F₀, making it nonparametric. Uniform(0,1) has cdf 0 below zero, x within the interval, and 1 above one. A mean or median test cannot distinguish all nonuniform alternatives sharing those summaries. Estimated model parameters change the usual KS calibration. Checking random number generators also requires tests for serial dependence, since a uniform marginal distribution alone does not guarantee independent observations.',[('What KS statistic tests standard normal fit?','supₓ|Fₙ(x)−Φ(x)|, using the specified normal cdf.'),('Can a uniform histogram alone prove independence?','No: repeating the same uniform draw at every time gives uniform marginals but complete dependence.')],track='Statistics',difficulty='Easy')
add(60,'Probability Distributions','Y is exponential with mean θ and X|Y=y∼N(0,y). Find φX(t)=E[eⁱᵗˣ].',['exp(−θt²/2)','exp(−t²/(2θ))','1/(1+θt²/2)','θ/(θ+θt²/2)'],'C',['The conditional characteristic function is exp(−yt²/2).','Average this using the exponential Laplace transform E[e⁻ˢʸ]=1/(1+θs).'],'By conditional expectation, φX(t)=E[e⁻ʸᵗ²ᐟ²]=∫₀∞e⁻ʸᵗ²ᐟ² θ⁻¹e⁻ʸᐟθdy=1/(1+θt²/2), C. A would correspond to fixed variance θ, B to fixed variance 1/θ, and D cancels θ and therefore fails the variance check −φ″(0)=θ except when θ=1. The mixture is a centered Laplace distribution with scale √(θ/2).','The characteristic function of N(μ,σ²) is exp(iμt−σ²t²/2). For a conditional normal distribution, substitute the conditional parameters and then average using the law of total expectation. Randomizing the variance produces a scale mixture of normals, typically not another normal law. The exponential distribution with mean θ has Laplace transform 1/(1+θs), s≥0. Combining these transforms yields a rational characteristic function, characteristic of a symmetric Laplace distribution here. Derivatives at zero provide useful checks: φ(0)=1, φ′(0)=iE[X], and −φ″(0)=E[X²] when the moments exist. Parameterization by mean versus rate must be handled consistently.',[('Y∼Exp(mean 2),X|Y∼N(0,Y). Find φX.','1/(1+t²), giving Var(X)=2.'),('For X|Y∼N(a,Y) with the same mixing law, find φX.','eⁱᵃᵗ/(1+θt²/2); the constant mean adds the phase factor.')],track='Statistics')
